     "type": "concept",
     "title": "The Clinical Tale as a Genre",
     "content": "Sacks revived a nearly lost form: the **clinical tale** — a detailed, narrative case history that treats the patient as a full human being, not a list of symptoms.\n\nThe genre has deep roots:\n• **Hippocrates** wrote case histories as unfolding stories of illness\n• **Nineteenth-century neurology** (Charcot, Head, Weir Mitchell) blended close observation with vivid description\n• **Twentieth-century medicine** drifted toward the impersonal lab report — patient reduced to numbers\n\nSacks complained that the modern case history is **'a kind of natural history'** that tells us nothing about the individual. It mentions the disease but never the person who has it.\n\nHis solution was to write the **'who'** back in. A clinical tale keeps the rigor of diagnosis but restores the patient's voice, world, and selfhood. It treats neurological disorder as a drama played out in an individual life — with losses, adaptations, and sometimes unexpected gifts.",
