     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Luria and the Birth of Romantic Science",
     "content": "Sacks's deepest influence was the Soviet neuropsychologist **A. R. Luria** (1902-1977). Luria distinguished two scientific styles:\n\n• **Classical science** — breaks phenomena into elements, abstracts laws, reduces the particular to the general. Powerful, but it can lose the living whole.\n• **Romantic science** — preserves the richness and unity of real events, describing the individual case in full without sacrificing rigor.\n\nLuria's own books — *The Mind of a Mnemonist* (about a man who could not forget) and *The Man with a Shattered World* (a soldier rebuilding his mind after a bullet wound) — were extended portraits of single patients. They were science AND literature.\n\nSacks read these as a young man and wept. He wrote that Luria gave him **'permission'** to combine the neurologist's precision with the storyteller's compassion. *The Man Who Mistook His Wife for a Hat* is Sacks's own contribution to romantic science: clinical truth told as human drama.",
