    {
     "type": "application",
     "title": "Treating Conditions That Are Part of the Self",
     "content": "The Excesses teach a clinical ethic that reaches far beyond Tourette's. Apply these principles whenever a condition is entangled with identity:\n\n1. **Ask what the patient values** — before suppressing a trait, learn whether they experience it as intrusion or as part of who they are.\n\n2. **Separate harm from identity** — distinguish the ego-dystonic features that cause real damage from the ego-syntonic features the person treasures.\n\n3. **Titrate, don't obliterate** — aim for the lowest effective control of harm; full suppression can erase valued function (as haloperidol nearly erased Ray).\n\n4. **Use time and context** — like Ray's weekday/weekend plan, structure treatment so the trait is contained where it harms and free where it helps.\n\n5. **Respect autonomy** — the patient, like Natasha, may rationally choose to keep some 'excess.' Inform fully, then honor their valuation.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">TREATING THE EMBEDDED TRAIT</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Five principles</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"170\" width=\"800\" height=\"90\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"190\" y=\"205\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">1.</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"205\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"19\">Ask what the patient values</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">intrusion or identity?</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"280\" width=\"800\" height=\"90\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"190\" y=\"315\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">2.</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"315\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"19\">Separate harm from identity</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"350\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">dystonic vs syntonic features</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"390\" width=\"800\" height=\"90\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"190\" y=\"425\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">3.</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"425\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"19\">Titrate, don't obliterate</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"460\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">lowest effective control</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"500\" width=\"800\" height=\"90\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"190\" y=\"535\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">4.</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"535\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"19\">Use time and context</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"570\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">contain where it harms, free where it helps</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"610\" width=\"800\" height=\"90\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(236,72,153,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ec4899\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"190\" y=\"645\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#ec4899\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">5.</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"645\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"19\">Respect autonomy</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"680\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">honor the patient's valuation</text></svg>",
      "caption": "An ethic for identity-entangled conditions"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "practice",
     "title": "Practice: Negotiate a Treatment Plan",
     "content": "**Exercise.** A 24-year-old visual artist with Tourette's reports that her tics embarrass her at her office job but that her 'restless, jumpy energy' fuels her painting, which she does in the evenings and on weekends. She is reluctant to start a tic-suppressing medication because she fears 'losing the spark.' Drawing on Ray's case, propose a plan and justify it.\n\n**Model answer.** Propose a **context-structured, titrated approach** modeled on Ray. Offer the lowest effective dose of a tic-suppressing agent (or a non-pharmacologic option like CBIT — Comprehensive Behavioral Intervention for Tics — see next card) timed and dosed to cover the workday, leaving evenings/weekends comparatively unmedicated so her creative energy is preserved for painting. Validate that her 'jumpy energy' is ego-syntonic and worth protecting, while the workplace tics are ego-dystonic harm to be reduced. Set explicit shared goals (reduce workplace disruption; preserve creative drive), monitor for the flat, 'erased' feeling Ray experienced, and adjust. The justification: this separates harm from identity, titrates rather than obliterates, uses time/context, and respects her autonomy — the exact ethic the Excesses teach.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">PRACTICE: NEGOTIATE</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">A Ray-style plan for a new patient</text><rect x=\"120\" y=\"200\" width=\"860\" height=\"120\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"248\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">HARM (dystonic)</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"290\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">workplace tics - reduce these</text><rect x=\"120\" y=\"350\" width=\"860\" height=\"120\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"398\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">GIFT (syntonic)</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"440\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">creative energy - protect this</text><rect x=\"120\" y=\"500\" width=\"860\" height=\"120\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"548\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">PLAN</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"590\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">titrate + time to workday, monitor</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"650\" width=\"700\" height=\"80\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"698\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-size=\"20\">Separate harm from identity, respect autonomy</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Applying the Excesses ethic"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "practice",
     "title": "Practice: Distinguish Excess from Deficit",
     "content": "**Exercise.** Sort each presentation as a disorder of **excess (too much)** or **deficit/loss (too little)**, and name the broad mechanism (disinhibition vs. loss of function). A. A child with sudden, repetitive vocal and motor tics that build an urge if suppressed. B. A stroke patient who cannot move the right arm at all. C. An elderly man with new euphoria, grandiosity, and heightened libido found to have neurosyphilis. D. A patient who, after a brain injury, has lost the ability to feel where her limbs are.\n\n**Model answer.** A = **Excess** (Tourette's; disinhibition of CSTC circuits — too much movement/impulse). B = **Deficit** (motor loss from corticospinal damage — too little function). C = **Excess** (general paresis; cortical disinhibition — too much drive/appetite). D = **Deficit** (proprioceptive loss — too little sensory feedback). Mechanistically, A and C are failures of *inhibition/the brake*, producing surplus; B and D are *loss of function/input*, producing absence. Sacks's two-part division (Losses vs Excesses) is precisely this contrast — and recognizing which side a syndrome sits on shapes both diagnosis and the goal of treatment.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">PRACTICE: SORT THEM</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Excess or deficit?</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"190\" width=\"440\" height=\"220\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">EXCESS</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"290\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">A - tics (Tourette's)</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"325\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">C - neurosyphilis</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"375\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">brake fails - surplus</text><rect x=\"580\" y=\"190\" width=\"440\" height=\"220\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">DEFICIT</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"290\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">B - paralysis (stroke)</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"325\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"18\">D - lost proprioception</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"375\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"16\">function lost - absence</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"450\" width=\"700\" height=\"200\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"510\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-size=\"22\">Inhibition failure vs function loss</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"560\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"19\">Sacks's Losses vs Excesses</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"605\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">which side shapes the treatment goal</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Reading the direction of the disorder"
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "quiz",
     "question": "What is the leading neurobiological account of tic generation in Tourette syndrome?",
     "options": [
      {
       "text": "Demyelination of peripheral nerves",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "Dysfunction of cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical circuits with relative dopaminergic overactivity, causing disinhibition of movements/impulses",
       "correct": true
      },
      {
       "text": "Loss of dopamine neurons in the substantia nigra, as in Parkinson's",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "Thiamine deficiency damaging the mammillary bodies",
       "correct": false
      }
     ],
     "explanation": "Tourette's is understood as a disorder of the cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical (CSTC) loops that gate movement and impulse, with relative dopaminergic overactivity weakening normal inhibition — hence tics 'break through.' This is why dopamine-blocking drugs like haloperidol suppress tics. It is a central, not peripheral, process (not demyelination); it is dopamine EXCESS/dysregulation, the opposite of Parkinson's nigral dopamine LOSS; and it has nothing to do with thiamine deficiency (that is Korsakoff).",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">KNOWLEDGE CHECK</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Mechanism of tics</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"220\" width=\"800\" height=\"200\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.1)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"285\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">CSTC + DOPAMINE EXCESS</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"340\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">disinhibition releases movements</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"385\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">haloperidol blocks dopamine, suppresses tics</text><rect x=\"250\" y=\"470\" width=\"600\" height=\"90\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"525\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-size=\"20\">Which mechanism is correct?</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Confirm the neurobiology of Tourette's"
     }
    }
